# This is the one scenario that needs a REAL shm zone whose name contains
# characters ngx_test_probe_escape_json_string() has to escape -- see
# t/module/ngx_http_test_ref_module.c's file header for why the reference
# module takes no zone by default and why `test_ref_zone` is the opt-in.
#
# PROBER_PROBE overrides mutate-suite-lib.sh's default ("test_ref_probe;"):
# the probe directive is unchanged, but this scenario ALSO needs
# `test_ref_zone` in the http block, which only nginx.conf's @PROBE_ZONE@
# placeholder reaches. PROBER_PROBE_ZONE supplies that.
#
# The zone name is an nginx-conf double-quoted string, unescaped by nginx's
# own lexer (ngx_conf_read_token) before ngx_http_test_ref_zone_directive
# ever sees it -- this file does not do any escaping of its own, it just
# writes the nginx-conf source form. It carries, in order:
#   - a literal double quote                    (\")
#   - a literal backslash                        (\\)
#   - a literal ASCII TAB, 0x09                   (\t)
# which cover all three families ngx_test_probe_escape_json_string switches
# on: the two characters JSON forbids unescaped (" and \), and a C0 control
# character that gets the \u00XX form. A literal LF or CR cannot be used here:
# ngx_conf_read_token treats an embedded (unescaped) newline as an ordinary
# token character but the shell/heredoc plumbing that gets this string from
# nginx.conf to the process is not newline-safe across all supported
# platforms, so \t stands in as the representative control character -- the
# escape helper does not special-case which control byte it is (see its
# `c < 0x20` branch), so \t exercises the exact same code path \n or \r would.
PROBER_PROBE="test_ref_probe;"
PROBER_PROBE_ZONE='test_ref_zone "a\"b\\c\td" 1m;'
